---

My pantry has slowly turned over this year. Not all at once, but staple by staple, until I looked up and realized half of what I reach for weekly wasn't in the house a year ago. Here's what's actually stuck around, and why.

## Lentils and lentil pasta

Lentils were the first real swap. They cook fast, they don't need a soak like most dried beans, and they hold their shape well enough to go in soups, curries, or just tossed with olive oil and whatever vegetable is around. Lentil pasta has been the bigger surprise though — it looks and cooks almost exactly like regular pasta, but the extra protein and fiber mean it doesn't spike the way a bowl of regular noodles does. It's not a perfect stand-in for every dish, but for a weeknight red sauce pasta it's close enough that nobody's complained.

## Whole wheat and oat flour

I still keep white flour around for the things that genuinely need it, but whole wheat and oat flour have taken over most everyday baking. Oat flour in particular has been a good one for pancakes and quick breads — a little denser, a little more filling, and it plays nicer with blood sugar than a stack of white-flour pancakes ever did. Whole wheat flour is the one I default to now unless a recipe really depends on a lighter crumb.

## Monk fruit, two ways

This is the pair I didn't expect to like as much as I do. Monk fruit granules are a straight 1:1 swap for sugar in most baking, which matters a lot — no recalculating a recipe, no guessing at ratios, just swap it in. The monk fruit drops are the one I reach for outside of baking, in coffee or tea or anywhere I want sweetness without committing to an actual dessert. It's about as clean an option as I've found — no sugar alcohols to deal with, no aftertaste I notice.

## Chia, flax, millet, and injera flour

These four came in more gradually. Chia and flax seeds go into almost anything — oatmeal, smoothies, baked goods — mostly for the fiber, which has made a real difference for me. I also use millet in some things such as adding it to soups or in my morning gronala. Injera flour is the newest addition, and honestly the most fun — it's teff-based, and making injera at home has turned into its own small project rather than a chore, its a great alernative to bread or tortillas sometimes.

## Rolled oats, still

Rolled oats never left, but they're worth mentioning because they're the one staple all of the above tends to build around — a base for overnight oats mixed with chia and flax, ground into oat flour when I need it, or just a plain bowl on the mornings that don't call for anything fancier.

None of this is a complete pantry, and it's still changing. But this is the list I'd hand someone if they asked what's actually different about how I stock the shelves now compared to a year ago.
